is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Router16 is alive and forwarding normally. Evidence: from the data center host dc_pc5 (10.5.0.5), pings to multiple Campus 1 subnets succeed with 0% loss (e.g., 172.16.10.5, 172.16.20.10, 172.16.30.12). Router16’s control plane is healthy: OSPF adjacencies are Full both upstream to router7 on eth2 (172.30.1.1↔172.30.1.2) and downstream toward the campus core on bond0 (10.2.2.1↔10.2.2.2). Router16’s routing table contains OSPF-learned routes to 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, and 172.16.30.0/24 via 10.2.2.2, and router7’s table points those same prefixes via 172.30.1.1 (router16), confirming end-to-end reachability across the edge. Additional cross-site probes (e.g., pc3→172.16.10.5) also succeed.

Mechanism: If router16 had “crashed,” its OSPF neighbors would be down, upstream routers would withdraw Campus 1 routes, and inter-building pings would fail. Instead, adjacencies are up, routes are installed, and traffic passes with normal RTTs. Localization: no faulty interface or daemon is observed on router16; both eth2 and bond0 participate in OSPF and are exchanging LSAs.

Root cause: The reported outage appears to be a false alarm or a transient condition that has already cleared.

Corrective fix: No reboot is required. Router16 is operating correctly. If users still report intermittent issues, capture time-correlated traceroutes from affected hosts and review OSPF/interface logs on router16 and router7 for brief flaps; otherwise, no changes are needed. Communicate status back to Helpdesk and close the ticket as not reproducible.
